# Small alternative: reviewed historical discovery catalog, routing only

The one-call availability observation returned transformed HTML text but **zero literal HTML link edges**. Its only SDK URL hint is `/info/bcp14/`, with unknown role. It did not forward the historical info→Plain Text navigation edge. No target was followed and no alias/witness/current-origin credit is available. This bounded negative result does not establish that Exa can never forward useful links, or that another claim needs more factual evidence.

The smallest general alternative is a separately installed, admin-reviewed **historical discovery-edge catalog**. It may suggest a candidate URL from a genuinely observed source link when that exact parent URL already appears in an admitted public retrieval. It must not map claim regexes, answer values or receipt targets to desired evidence. It does not change which statement was admitted or grant source identity, assessment, independence, cache validity or authority.

There is one concrete eligible example in already saved origin material: the RFC info page's literal `href="/rfc/rfc8174.txt"` labeled `Plain Text`, recorded at05:01:27–28 with bodySHA256 `c42a37389997b2e93398362045a02afeef5c93d912658037ba5f41c307c04d4f`. The same body self-canonicalizes to `/info/rfc8174/`. This proves a historical source-explicit format route; it does not make the two URLs interchangeable. The related RSS alternate and citation TXT link are counterexamples to inferring a document route from `alternate`, `.txt` or `TXT` alone.

A catalog entry should retain an installation version/generation, exact parent URL, dated source observation, complete source-body hash/location, exact href/anchor spans and values, resolved target URL, observed role and any explicit edition label. The installer rechecks bytes/spans and records review/revocation. The only permitted use is `candidate_routing_only`; identity grants are empty. Match the parent URL exactly—no invented path rewrite, alias table or claim-to-answer lookup. The catalog should include only edges supported by its saved source, not arbitrary installed canonical receipt URLs.

At runtime, a historical edge keyed by the observed parent URL can be an **advisory stale routing hint**, even though the current SDK-transformed parent body does not match the old origin body hash. Requiring that exact old parent hash would correctly leave this example unusable without another origin reader. Therefore the catalog must state what it relaxes: route suggestion from a dated observation, not proof the link remains current. Any suggested target stays a separate record. Its actual response URL, version, full unit/quote alignment, source proof and ordinary policy blockers must independently pass the witness/assessment path. Redirects, freshness, legal current force or receipt equivalence cannot be inherited from the catalog. A stale or wrong target simply remains unresolved.

Keep source/header/metadata provenance distinct. A reviewed edge is not a reviewed factual answer. A dated request must not silently follow a latest-edition route, and an asserted publication date/value must not be mistaken for an edition selector. Ambiguous scope remains unresolved; generic raw-English routing is still an integration prerequisite. Revoking/replacing the catalog or its source observations must invalidate outstanding routing proposals through the runtime owner/generation checks, not just change a title or cache key.

## Budget feasibility and required guard change

A catalog lookup itself is local and costs no search/Jev request. One proposed target could consume one of the **existing three contents attempts and original10s**, with retries consuming those same slots; no deadline reset, extra allowance, recursive second hop or direct website fallback is allowed. In the earlier neutral RFC run all three contents slots were already spent. Appending a fourth is forbidden. Reserving a slot for the catalog target would displace another enrichment/companion, so full-pool and contradiction/spillover audits are required. The six relevant factual RFC replays already retain the complete definition; there is no demonstrated missing-premise gain justifying an automatic extra request.

Existing guards allow contents only for URLs returned by that search. A catalog target may not be one of them. A future integration therefore needs an explicit, reviewed **source-bound routing reason** accepted by the egress observer before reservation: exact observed parent URL, installed catalog hash/generation, audited edge and target, same active admitted claim/session owner, and remaining budget. It must not bypass the URL allowlist merely because a caller supplies a constructible Graph or a familiar host. No such production integration is implemented here.

At the latest observed provider-returned rate, one contents operation was$0.001; earlier calls returned$0.002. Those are observations, not future pricing guarantees. A child request can fail, retry within the cap, exhaust time, return another extraction or fail exact unit binding. Canonical target availability and factual/latency gain remain unmeasured. It does not establish a fresh canonical reader: Exa may still return cached transformed material.

For other families, do not invent routes. A genuine section→FullText link would need a captured source supporting that direction; a first-release→edition link needs its actual source/edition labels. The saved ONS neutral-query wrong edition is not proof of production factual-query failure. Pulitzer already returned its canonical URL, so a catalog does not solve its lead/rendering/unit problem. No aliases, inferred reverse edges or source acquisition are authorized by this note.

A useful next step would be an offline catalog proposal/validation experiment only: genuine RFC edge plus synthetic dated/latest, citation-versus-document, ambiguous/missing link role, source-hash mismatch, revoked generation, unsafe/external target, duplicate/cycle and exhausted-budget controls. Test wrong compared values without changing the route. The target must still independently fail or pass exact witness checks, and every original companion/failure stays visible. A later live plan would require separate review and authorization. No new catalog install, provider call, production routing or readiness claim follows from this proposal.
